<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/21st-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yemeni civil war (2014–present)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2014">2014</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a> · Also known as Yemeni civil war · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yemeni civil war began in 2014 when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/houthi-movement">Houthi movement</a> seized Sanaa and then forced President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-rabbuh-mansur-hadi">Abd Rabbuh Mansur Hadi</a> from power. In 2015 a coalition led by Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> intervened in support of the government. The war split the country among the Houthis, the government and southern separatists and caused one of the worst humanitarian crises of the century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yemeni-civil-war-2014-present.jpg" alt="Yemeni civil war (2014–present)" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ali Zifan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yemeni_Civil_War.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> takes mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> and Shia forms, and among the Shia the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> and Zaydi sects historically competed for influence. After the Ayyubid expansion most of the Hamdan tribe converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaydism">Zaydism</a>, and during the Rasulid period, from 1229 to 1454, Shafi'i Sunni Islam gained popularity. From the 1960s Zaydis faced discrimination under governments that favoured Sunni Islam. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/houthi-movement">Houthis</a>, or Ansar Allah, are a Zaydi group from the Sa'dah Governorate on Yemen's northern border with Saudi Arabia, who led a low-level insurgency against the government from 2004 after their leader, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussein-badreddin-al-houthi">Hussein Badreddin al-Houthi</a>, was killed in a military crackdown.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>After weeks of street protests against the administration of President Abdrabbuh Mansur Hadi, which had cut fuel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/houthi-movement">Houthis</a> fought army forces under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-mohsen-al-ahmar">Ali Mohsen al-Ahmar</a> and in a battle of a few days took Sanaa in September 2014. Hadi negotiated an agreement under which the government resigned and the Houthis gained more influence over state institutions than ever before.</p><p>On 21 March 2015 the Houthi-led Supreme Revolutionary Committee declared a general mobilisation. With forces loyal to the former president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-abdullah-saleh">Ali Abdullah Saleh</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/houthi-movement">Houthis</a> fought in Lahij Governorate and by 25 March had reached the outskirts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>, where Hadi's government sat. Hadi fled the country the same day. A coalition led by Saudi Arabia began air strikes and other operations to restore the government. Aden was recaptured, but the conflict settled into a protracted stalemate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Saleh was assassinated in 2017 after his alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/houthi-movement">Houthis</a> broke down. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-transitional-council">Southern Transitional Council</a>, formed in 2017 and backed by the United Arab Emirates, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> in 2018, and clashes followed between it and forces loyal to Hadi, who were backed by Saudi Arabia. The Stockholm Agreement of December 2018 sought a ceasefire around Hodeida. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> truce began in April 2022.</p><p>The war brought a severe humanitarian crisis. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> reports more than 150,000 people killed, and estimates of more than 227,000 further deaths from famine and the lack of health care. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> meets 90% of its food needs through imports, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> disrupted deliveries, while cholera cases rose from 2015. The UN Security Council imposed an arms embargo on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/houthi-movement">Houthis</a> in 2015.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yemeni civil war (2014–present)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
